✅ @Transactional in Spring – Complete Explanation with Example
@Transactional is an annotation in Spring used to manage database transactions.

It ensures that:

All operations within a method run in a single transaction

If any part fails or throws an exception, all changes are rolled back

If everything succeeds, the transaction is committed

3️⃣ Spring Boot @Transactional – Deep Flow
🔁 What happens internally?
@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    debit();
    credit();
}

Behind the scenes 🧠
Spring opens DB transaction
Auto-commit = OFF
Executes all SQL
If no exception → COMMIT
If RuntimeException → ROLLBACK
DB ensures ACID

🔍 Where is @Transactional Used?
On service layer methods (recommended)

Can also be applied on class level (all methods inside will be transactional)

✅ Common Use Case

@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Autowired
    private AccountRepository accountRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void registerUser(User user, Account account) {
        userRepository.save(user);            // Step 1
        accountRepository.save(account);      // Step 2

        // If any exception is thrown here, both save operations are rolled back
    }
}
⚠ Without @Transactional
If Step 1 succeeds and Step 2 fails, Step 1 remains committed, causing data inconsistency.

🧠 What Happens Behind the Scenes?
Spring opens a transaction before method runs.

Executes method logic.

If everything is OK → COMMIT.

If an exception occurs → ROLLBACK.

✅ Key Options of @Transactional
| Attribute     | Description                                                     |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `readOnly`    | Optimize for read-only queries (no write allowed)               |
| `rollbackFor` | Define which exceptions should trigger rollback                 |
| `propagation` | Defines transaction behavior when already in a transaction      |
| `isolation`   | Controls how transaction handles concurrency (read/write locks) |
| `timeout`     | Transaction time limit (in seconds)                             |


✅ Example with Attributes

@Transactional(
    readOnly = false,
    rollbackFor = Exception.class,
    propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED,
    isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED,
    timeout = 30
)
public void updateData() {
    // your transactional logic
}

⚙️ @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)

By default, Spring rolls back only for unchecked exceptions (i.e. subclasses of RuntimeException and Error).
But sometimes you also want to roll back for checked exceptions (like IOException, SQLException, etc.).

So:

@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)

means:

Roll back the transaction for any type of Exception, both checked and unchecked.

✅ Example:

@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void processPayment() throws Exception {
    saveTransaction();
    if (someConditionFails) {
        throw new Exception("Something went wrong");
    }
}


Without rollbackFor, the transaction would not roll back because Exception is checked.
With it, Spring ensures the whole transaction is rolled back.

📖 @Transactional(readOnly = true)

This tells Spring (and the underlying database connection) that this transaction is read-only — you are not going to modify data.

It’s typically used for queries, reporting, or fetching data.

Spring can optimize such transactions — for example:

It might skip dirty checking in Hibernate (less overhead).

The database might enforce or optimize read-only behavior.

✅ Example:

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<User> getAllUsers() {
    return userRepository.findAll();
}

If you accidentally try to perform an update inside this method, some databases might throw an exception, or Spring might not flush the persistence context.

🧠 Summary Table
| Attribute                       | Purpose                                                    | Example                    |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `rollbackFor = Exception.class` | Roll back for both checked & unchecked exceptions          | For full rollback coverage |
| `readOnly = true`               | Optimize for read operations; no data modification allowed | Use for `SELECT` queries   |


✅ Propagation Types (Important in Microservices & Nested Calls)
| Type           | Meaning                                                                |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `REQUIRED`     | Default. Join existing transaction or create a new one if none exists. |
| `REQUIRES_NEW` | Always create a new transaction, suspend existing.                     |
| `NESTED`       | Start nested transaction within current one (requires special setup).  |
| `SUPPORTS`     | Join existing transaction if available.                                |


✅ Best Practices
Use on service methods, not on DAOs or controllers.

Avoid placing it on private methods (proxy-based AOP won't work).

Avoid using it on interfaces if using Spring’s proxy approach.

🧪 Testing @Transactional
If you annotate a test with @Transactional, Spring will roll back all changes made during the test after it finishes.

@SpringBootTest
@Transactional
public class MyServiceTest {
    @Test
    public void testSomething() {
        // will rollback after test
    }
}


🔍 @Transactional: jakarta.transaction vs javax.transaction

✅ Short Answer:
| Old (Pre Java EE 9 / Spring Boot < 3) | New (Spring Boot 3+ / Jakarta EE 9+) |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `javax.transaction.Transactional`     | `jakarta.transaction.Transactional`  |


✅ Why the Change?
In Spring Boot 3 and Jakarta EE 9, all Java EE packages were migrated from javax.* to jakarta.*.

This change was made because Oracle transferred the Java EE namespace to the Eclipse Foundation (Jakarta EE), and due to licensing, the package names had to change.

✅ Imports Based on Version:
| Scenario                       | You Import From                                                          |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Spring Boot **2.x**            | `javax.transaction.Transactional`                                        |
| Spring Boot **3.x+**           | `jakarta.transaction.Transactional`                                      |
| Using **Spring’s own** version | `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional` ✅ RECOMMENDED |


✅ Which One Should You Use?
✔️ Always prefer:

import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
Because:

It works across all versions

It supports Spring-specific features like rollbackFor, propagation, etc.

It’s more commonly used in Spring Boot apps

✅ Comparison
| Feature              | `jakarta.transaction.Transactional` | `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional` |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| Comes from           | Jakarta EE                          | Spring Framework                                           |
| Attributes supported | Limited                             | Full support (rollbackFor, propagation, isolation, etc.)   |
| Version flexibility  | Jakarta EE only                     | Spring-specific, very flexible                             |


✅ Example (Recommended for Spring Boot)

import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class UserService {

    @Transactional
    public void registerUser(User user) {
        // Transactional code here
    }
}
✅ In Summary:
| Use Case                        | Recommended Import                                           |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Using **Spring Boot**           | `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional` ✅ |
| Strict **Jakarta EE** only      | `jakarta.transaction.Transactional`                          |
| Using **older Spring Boot 2.x** | `javax.transaction.Transactional`                            |
